ven-names></name><name><surname>Hanney</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name></person-group><year>1996</year><article-title>How can payback from health services research be assessed?</article-title><source>J Health Serv Res Policy</source><volume>1</volume><fpage>35</fpage><lpage>43</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10180843</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b5-ehp0116-000583"><citation citation-type="book"><collab>Centers for Disease Control and Prevention</collab><year>2005</year><source>Introduction to Program Evaluation for Public Health Programs: A Self-Study Guide</source><publisher-loc>Atlanta, GA</publisher-loc><publisher-name>U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="b6-ehp0116-000583"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Chen</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name></person-group><year>2005</year><source>Practical Program Evaluation: Assessing and Improving Planning, Implementation, and Effectiveness</source><publisher-loc>Thousand Oaks, CA</publisher-loc><publisher-name>Sage Publications</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="b7-ehp0116-000583"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Cozzens</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name></person-group><year>1997</year><article-title>The knowledge pool: measurement challenges in evaluating fundamental research programs</article-title><source>Eval Program Plann</source><volume>1</volume><fpage>77</fpage><lpage>89</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="b8-ehp0116-000583"><citation citation-type="web"><collab>Department of Health and Human Services</collab><year>2002</year><article-title>HHS helps in efforts to eliminate childhood lead poisoning. U.S. Department of Health and Human Services</article-title><comment>Available: <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://www.hhs.gov/news/press/2002pres/lead.html">http://www.hhs.gov/news/press/2002pres/lead.